WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.090
(thunder booms)

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:02.410
- [Narrator] Normally when a hurricane

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:07.410
is barrelling towards us, we
tend to run the other way.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.560
But not Isla, she's a leatherback turtle

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.220
who was meandering off
the coast of Virginia

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.790
when she accidentally swam
straight into Hurricane Florence.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.840
Scientists were worried
she'd get caught up

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.160
in the middle of the storm,
but Isla managed to survive

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.180
by swimming towards deeper waters.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.910
Turns out there's a part of hurricanes

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:32.840
we don't often think about:
what happens under the surface.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.749
And Isla is just one example.

00:00:35.749 --> 00:00:38.666
(compelling music)

00:00:41.869 --> 00:00:44.580
Far out at sea, fish that
live near the surface

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.970
might feel some turbulence
as a storm passes,

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:52.110
but most sea creatures including
dolphins, whales and sharks

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:56.540
avoid the rough surface water
and swim to calmer seas.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.600
But it's a different story near shore.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.130
Changes in water temperature

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:04.620
and salinity can be
catastrophic for marine life.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.640
Hurricanes can generate massive waves,

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.010
which mix warm surface water

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.670
with cooler, saltier water
below, generating currents

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:17.410
that extend up to 91
meters below the surface.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.130
These currents are so strong,

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:23.720
that they can sweep manatees
inland into canals and ponds,

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.170
or away from coastal waters altogether

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.340
and into the open ocean,

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.410
where they can become
disoriented and even die.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.570
Hurricanes also bring heavy rains,

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.870
so freshwater often floods coastal areas.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.930
And because freshwater is
less dense than saltwater,

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:45.360
it sits on top of the
saltwater like oil on vinegar,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.900
where it can prevent oxygen

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.090
from reaching the salty layer below

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.910
and disrupt salinity levels,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.950
which can lead to sores,
lesions and ultimately death

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.880
in whales, dolphins and porpoises.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.180
Hurricanes can also kick up
dirt and sand in shallow seas,

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:05.540
which can kill fish by
clogging their gills.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.420
Experts think that this is
probably one of the factors

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:13.170
that killed an estimated
9.4 million saltwater fish

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.690
in 1992 during Hurricane Andrew.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.630
The dirty, murky water
also blocks sunlight

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.100
from reaching corals and sea grass.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.520
In fact, scientists found that coral cover

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.130
in the Caribbean decreases on average

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:31.490
by 17% a year after a hurricane strikes.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.280
And that's in addition to the stress

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.660
coral already face from human interference

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.830
from things like global
warming or pollution.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:43.150
But hurricanes are not
always bad news for sea life,

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:44.020
believe it or not.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.660
After Hurricane Katrina
damaged or destroyed 90%

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.420
of fishing boats in the Mississippi Sound,

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.100
scientists observed a huge
increase in dolphin births.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.930
Without all the fishing boats around,

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:58.640
dolphins suddenly found themselves

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.940
with tons of fish all at their disposal,

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.080
and their populations thrived.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.980
And of course, hurricanes
impact land animals, too.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.200
Sometimes they change
ecosystems altogether.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.570
For instance, the Hawaiian island of Kauai

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:17.260
is now inundated with feral chickens.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.310
Locals say they are the descendants

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.570
of domesticated chickens that escaped

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.100
when hurricanes blew open coops.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.450
And in North Carolina, torrential rains

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.500
from Hurricane Florence overwhelmed

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.610
more than 100 hog waste lagoons,

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:36.590
possibly releasing pig waste
into the local water supply.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.830
Unfortunately, research indicates

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:42.000
that the intensity of
hurricanes will only increase

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.880
with climate change.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.760
So if we don't get a handle on it soon,

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.900
we'll be in some deep shh pig waste,

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.344
we'll be in some deep pig waste.

00:03:51.344 --> 00:03:54.261
(compelling music)

00:03:55.132 --> 00:03:57.337
(electronic tones)

